# What Vellum may NAME. The other half is vellum.caps, which says what it may
# DO; the kernel enforces both, and a grant here can only ever narrow what the
# session already holds.
#
# This file used to be comments only, which means INHERIT -- the browser could
# name the whole session namespace. It could not persist anything, so nothing
# was lost by that; now that it keeps a cookie jar and localStorage, the right
# move was not to widen it but to write the grant down. A browser that can name
# two places is strictly more confined than one that inherits everything, so
# gaining persistence made it LESS able to reach your files, not more.
#
# $HOME, not /data/apps/vellum. The session makes the app directory READ-ONLY
# on purpose -- an application rewriting its own installed files is what a
# package manager exists to prevent -- and a browser's cookies are the USER's
# data anyway, not the program's. The launcher expands $HOME to whoever is
# logged in, so this declaration never has to name a person.

ro /system                  # its font, and the documents that ship with the OS
rw $HOME/.vellum            # its own state: the cookie jar, localStorage
